That makes sense. 👍 You’re describing two separate creator-economy systems under the broader Freedom World vision: one for music artists and their songs, and another for game developers and their games. They can share the same philosophy of creator choice without sharing the same cryptocurrency or economic structure.

🎵 1. JADE — The Music Creator Economy

Your original idea for JADE is to give musicians another way to control the economic value of their work, rather than automatically surrendering a large portion of their rights or future earnings to a traditional music-industry arrangement.

The goal would be to give an artist the option to use JADE in connection with a song.

For example, an artist might want to:

* Retain ownership of their music and the rights they choose to keep.
* Establish a clearly defined way for listeners to support or purchase access to their work.
* Choose whether JADE has any role in the song’s economic model.
* Decide how their music is distributed and how they earn money.
* Avoid participating in JADE entirely if a different business model suits them better.

One important distinction: owning a cryptocurrency associated with a song does not, by itself, give someone ownership of the song or guarantee that the artist receives all its future revenue. JADE would need clearly defined rights, agreements, and payment mechanisms to deliver the outcome you envision.

The central idea is that the artist should have a choice—not be forced into the traditional model.

🎮 2. Freedom World Game Tokens — A Separate System

The game economy would be different. We would develop a separate token concept specifically for games, with its own rules and purpose.

A game developer could choose from several models:

Option	How the creator could earn
Traditional game sales	Sell the game or charge for access.
Free-to-play	Earn through optional purchases or other disclosed monetization.
Expansions and downloadable content	Sell additional levels, stories, characters, or other content.
Sponsorships	Work with companies that support the game or its development.
Optional game-specific token	Use a separately designed token if it provides a genuine benefit to the game.
Combined model	Combine compatible options according to the game’s needs.

A creator could use one model or combine several. No creator would have to use cryptocurrency to publish a game on Freedom World.

We would also keep the game-token system separate from JADE and Freedom Token. Developing a future game token would not activate or authorize the issuance of either of those other tokens.

📣 3. How We Introduce These Options

I agree with your approach: we shouldn’t try to persuade artists or game developers to choose a particular financial model.

Instead, Freedom World can explain the options and let creators make informed decisions.

The process could look like this:

1. Create: An artist makes music, or a developer creates a game.
2. Explore: The creator learns about available ownership, distribution, and monetization options.
3. Compare: Freedom World explains the benefits, costs, risks, and obligations of each option.
4. Choose: The creator selects the model that best fits their goals—or chooses none of the token options.
5. Build: The creator proceeds under clear written terms.

We could eventually produce separate educational commercials: one explaining JADE to musicians and another explaining the optional game-token system to game developers. The advertising should explain what each system actually does without promising appreciation, guaranteed income, or financial returns.

🌎 4. The Bigger Freedom World Principle

What ties these ideas together is a common philosophy:

Creators should have more choices about how they own, distribute, and earn from their work.

That philosophy can extend beyond music and games to other creative industries, while each industry retains its own appropriate legal, technical, and economic framework.

And it fits the creator program we’ve already been developing: people can learn, build, earn, grow their skills, establish independent businesses, and return to Freedom World whenever they want.

One distinction I’d preserve in the design

For JADE, we should establish precisely what you mean by an artist keeping the economic value of a song for its entire lifetime: do you mean retaining 100% of the song’s revenue, retaining ownership of the song and its copyrights, or both? Those are different rights, and defining them clearly will help us design the system around your original intention.

The same principle will apply to the game-token system, but its rules will be developed independently.